window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][30]={"university_physics_en:M30-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M30-L1 – Classical fields and the Klein–Gordon equation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Master: Quantum field theory and the Standard Model<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M10: special relativity; M13:\nvariational principle; M25: quantum mechanics · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M29&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive the equation of motion of\na real scalar field from a Lagrangian density and evaluate its\nrelativistic dispersion relation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A field assigns a physical quantity to every spacetime point. Unlike\nthe trajectory of one particle, field theory describes continuously\ndistributed degrees of freedom. In a relativistic model, space and time\nderivatives enter one Lorentz-invariant action. A free real scalar field\nprovides the simplest example.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>We use natural units\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hbar=c=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand signature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_{\\mu\\nu}=\\mathrm{diag}(+1,-1,-1,-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nan explicit convention change from M29. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msup><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L=\\frac12\\partial_\\mu\\phi\\,\\partial^\\mu\\phi-\\frac12m^2\\phi^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Euler–Lagrange equation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>∂<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_\\mu(\\partial\\mathcal L/\\partial(\\partial_\\mu\\phi))-\\partial\\mathcal L/\\partial\\phi=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubstitution yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>□<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\Box+m^2)\\phi=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>□<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Box=\\partial_t^2-\\nabla^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA plane wave\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>∝<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ω<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi\\propto e^{-i\\omega t+i\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nobeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\omega^2+|\\mathbf k|^2+m^2=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ω<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega^2=|\\mathbf k|^2+m^2\\qquad(\\hbar=c=1).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf k|=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>13<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.606<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=\\sqrt{13}\\approx3.606<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe two plotted curves show\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mi>k<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=\\sqrt{k^2+m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor different masses. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe rest frequency remains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhereas large momenta lead to approximately linear growth.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_255_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Dispersion relations for two free scalar fields; the more massive field starts at a higher rest frequency.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Dispersion relations for two free scalar\nfields; the more massive field starts at a higher rest\nfrequency.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Field mass changes the minimum frequency of a free relativistic\nmode.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What equation results when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What assumption is made by omitting interaction terms from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=|k|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the positive-frequency branch.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin natural units.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The scalar field is free and has no modeled self-scattering.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The classical field equation is not yet a quantum theory. The curves\nrepresent free plane waves rather than measured particle spectra.\nNatural units and metric signature must remain consistent across\nformulas.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The free scalar field provides the starting point for canonical field\nquantization in M30-L2. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/quantum-field-theory/\">Tong – Quantum Field Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 1: classical fields and Klein–Gordon equation; Subject relevance to lesson: M30-L1 – Classical fields and the Klein–Gordon equation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M30-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M30-L2 – Canonical quantization and Fock states<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Master: Quantum field theory and the Standard Model<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M30-L1: free field equation; M25:\nharmonic oscillator · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Describe field modes as quantum\noscillators and compute energy differences between Fock states.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>How do continuous field oscillations give rise to discrete particle\nexcitations? Under canonical quantization, each independent free mode\nbecomes a harmonic quantum oscillator. Particle numbers are occupation\nnumbers of these modes; a Fock state may contain several identical\nbosons in one mode.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>As in M30-L1, we use natural units\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hbar=c=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nhence the energy spacing of a free mode is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_{\\mathbf k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℏ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hbar<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwritten explicitly, it is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℏ<\/mi><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hbar\\omega_{\\mathbf k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn a finite volume, start with discrete momenta\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nEach mode obeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msubsup><mi>a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo>†<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[a_{\\mathbf k},a_{\\mathbf k}^\\dagger]=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo>†<\/mo><\/msubsup><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_{\\mathbf k}=a_{\\mathbf k}^\\dagger a_{\\mathbf k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe states\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|n\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhave occupation numbers\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=0,1,2,\\dots<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mo>†<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^\\dagger|n\\rangle=\\sqrt{n+1}\\,|n+1\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Hamiltonian of a free mode becomes\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_{\\mathbf k}=\\omega_{\\mathbf k}(N_{\\mathbf k}+\\tfrac12)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe zero-point term is finite for one oscillator; summing it over\ninfinitely many field modes requires separate treatment.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_n=\\left(n+\\frac12\\right)\\omega_{\\mathbf k},\\qquad E_{n+1}-E_n=\\omega_{\\mathbf k}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathbf k|=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe mode frequency is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_{\\mathbf k}=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first three levels are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_0=2.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_1=7.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_2=12.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram illustrates equal level spacings\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ω<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nits arrows represent an increased occupation number, not motion in\nspace.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_096_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four equally spaced energy levels of one quantized field mode with upward transitions.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four equally spaced energy levels of one\nquantized field mode with upward transitions.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>In natural units\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hbar=c=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neach additional excitation of a free bosonic mode costs energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>ω<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒌<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega_{\\mathbf k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mo>†<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^\\dagger<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nact on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|2\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the adjacent-level spacing for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the Fock vacuum?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>a<\/mi><mo>†<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mn>3<\/mn><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a^\\dagger|2\\rangle=\\sqrt3\\,|3\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Five energy units.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The state without particle excitations in the considered free\nmodes.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>This elementary Fock picture applies directly to free fields.\nIdentifying states is more subtle in interacting theories. An absolute\nvacuum energy density cannot be inferred from an unregulated sum of\nzero-point energies.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Quantized modes give rise to field correlations and time-ordered\npropagators. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/quantum-field-theory/\">Tong – Quantum Field Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 2: canonical quantization and free fields; Subject relevance to lesson: M30-L2 – Canonical quantization and Fock states · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M30-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M30-L3 – Propagators and microcausality<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Master: Quantum field theory and the Standard Model<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M30-L2: field operators; M10: light\ncones · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish time ordering from\nspacelike separation and interpret the causal content of a field\ncommutator.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Quantum field theory must account for both field correlations and\nrelativistic causal structure. In perturbation theory, a propagator\nencodes correlations. It is not the probability of a classical signal\nbeing sent from A to B.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For a free scalar field let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>T<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_F(x-y)=\\langle0|T\\phi(x)\\phi(y)|0\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe symbol\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\norders operators in time; in momentum space the Feynman expression is\nproportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i/(p^2-m^2+i\\varepsilon)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTime ordering alone does not establish causality. Locality requires\nbosonic field operators at spacelike-separated points to commute. Under\nour signature, spacelike means\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒙<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s^2=(\\Delta t)^2-|\\Delta\\mathbf x|^2&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus a possibly nonzero time-ordered correlator cannot be used for\nfaster-than-light signalling.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">for<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[\\phi(x),\\phi(y)]=0\\quad\\text{for}\\quad (x-y)^2&lt;0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For two events with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\Delta x|=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin units with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s^2=4-9=-5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThey are spacelike separated. The figure places an event outside the\nlight cone; the field commutator does not allow a causal signal between\nthese events.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_152_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Spacetime light-cone diagram in ct–x coordinates showing a spacelike-separated event outside the cone.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Spacetime light-cone diagram in ct–x\ncoordinates showing a spacelike-separated event outside the\ncone.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Spacelike separation rules out a light-speed or slower causal\nsignal between the marked events.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t=4,\\,\\Delta x=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntimelike or spacelike?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmean in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_F<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>F<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≠<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_F\\neq0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noutside the light cone not imply faster-than-light messaging?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>7<\/mn><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s^2=16-9=7&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\ntimelike.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It orders the field operators in time.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Microcausality restricts signalling through the commutator, not by\nrequiring every time-ordered correlator to vanish.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The displayed commutator relation concerns local bosonic fields;\nfermionic fields require the corresponding anticommutators. The\nschematic shows 1+1 dimensions rather than complete 3+1-dimensional\nspacetime.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Time-ordered correlators are ingredients in perturbative expansions\nand scattering amplitudes. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/quantum-field-theory/\">Tong – Quantum Field Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 2: causality and propagators; Subject relevance to lesson: M30-L3 – Propagators and microcausality · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M30-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M30-L4 – Interactions, Wick’s theorem, and scattering amplitudes<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Master: Quantum field theory and the Standard Model<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M30-L3: time-ordered correlators · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain the relation between an\ninteraction term, Wick contractions, and an elementary scattering\namplitude.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Observable scattering requires interactions. Quantum field theory\noften expands time-ordered products perturbatively in the interaction\nstrength. Wick’s theorem resolves time-ordered products of free fields\ninto normal-ordered terms and contractions; Feynman diagrams organize\nthe terms.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>As a model choose a real scalar field with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><msup><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mi>!<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{int}}=-\\lambda\\phi^4/4!<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe four-field product produces a local vertex with four legs. The\nfactor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>!<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4!<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncompensates for the number of relevant permutations in Wick\ncontractions. Under standard conventions the tree-level vertex is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-i\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso the contact contribution to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>→<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\to2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nscattering obeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℳ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i\\mathcal M=-i\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nComplete rates also require external-state normalization, phase space,\npossible symmetry factors, and additional diagrams where\nappropriate.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mi>λ<\/mi><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>!<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><msup><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>i<\/mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℳ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{int}}=-\\frac{\\lambda}{4!}\\phi^4,\\qquad i\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{tree}}=-i\\lambda.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For an illustrative dimensionless coupling\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda=0.20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe contact term gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℳ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.040<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathcal M_{\\mathrm{tree}}|^2=0.040<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis number is <strong>not<\/strong> a cross section. The drawing shows\nfour lines meeting at a contact vertex; it is a combinatorial\nrepresentation, not a particle trajectory.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_256_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four incoming and outgoing field lines meet at a contact point labeled λ.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four incoming and outgoing field lines\nmeet at a contact point labeled λ.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>The local\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninteraction produces a four-point vertex.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many legs meet at a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\phi^4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncontact vertex?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℳ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\mathcal M|^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles at tree level?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is needed besides the amplitude to compute a cross\nsection?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Four.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It increases by a factor of four.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At least integration over the allowed phase space with the\nappropriate flux and normalization.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The simple tree amplitude applies to the given interaction at leading\nperturbative order. Physical cross sections depend on energy and the\nmodel. Diagrams denote mathematical contributions to amplitudes, not\nphotographs of microscopic paths.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Higher orders introduce loop integrals; their scale dependence\nmotivates renormalization and effective theories. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/quantum-field-theory/\">Tong – Quantum Field Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 3: Wick’s theorem, Feynman rules, and scattering; Subject relevance to lesson: M30-L4 – Interactions, Wick’s theorem, and scattering amplitudes · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M30-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M30-L5 – Path integrals, renormalization, and effective field theories<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Master: Quantum field theory and the Standard Model<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M30-L4: perturbative diagrams and\namplitudes · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Interpret the path integral as a\nweighted sum over field configurations and estimate suppression of\nhigher-dimensional operators.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>How can a theory work at low energy even when microscopic details are\nunknown? Path integrals provide an alternative description of field\ncorrelations. Effective field theory separates low-energy observables\nfrom a high scale at which additional degrees of freedom become\nimportant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Formally, the generating functional is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>J<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒟<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>J<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z[J]=\\int\\!\\mathcal D\\phi\\,e^{i(S[\\phi]+\\int J\\phi)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis notation requires a suitable regularization and definition of the\nintegration measure. An effective Lagrangian orders local operators by\nmass dimension. In four spacetime dimensions, a dimension-six operator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒪<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal O_6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas coefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c_6/\\Lambda^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt characteristic energy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>≪<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E\\ll\\Lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nits amplitude-like contribution is typically suppressed by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(E/\\Lambda)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrelative to dimension-four interactions, assuming no unusually large\ndimensionless coefficients or matrix elements. The renormalization group\ntracks how parameters run with the evaluation scale.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mrow><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mfrac><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒪<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>⋯<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L_{\\mathrm{eff}}=\\mathcal L_{\\leq4}+\\frac{c_6}{\\Lambda^2}\\mathcal O_6+\\cdots.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E/\\Lambda=0.10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe expected dimensionless suppression factor for this amplitude\ncontribution is about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.01<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram contrasts scales\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>E<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nit does not claim every observable rate decreases by exactly one\nhundredth. Interference with leading contributions can change observable\nscaling.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_077_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"High- and low-energy scales with a large ultraviolet block and a small low-energy correction.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">High- and low-energy scales with a large\nultraviolet block and a small low-energy correction.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Higher-dimensional operators yield controlled small corrections\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>≪<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E\\ll\\Lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen EFT assumptions hold.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>E<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(E/\\Lambda)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Lambda=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a dimension-six operator accompanied by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Lambda^{-2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is a loop-corrected renormalized coupling generally scale\nindependent?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.01<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">To give the Lagrangian density mass dimension four in four\ndimensions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No; a renormalized coupling generally runs with the renormalization\nscale.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Path integrals here are formal expressions; the compact notation does\nnot resolve mathematical existence questions. An effective theory\napplies below its assumed cutoff, with a sufficiently controlled\nexpansion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The method extends to relativistic spinor fields and electromagnetic\ngauge couplings. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/dbs26/AQFT.html\">Skinner – Quantum Field Theory II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Course outline: path integrals, Wilsonian EFT, and renormalization group; Subject relevance to lesson: M30-L5 – Path integrals, renormalization, and effective field theories · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/quantum-field-theory/\">Tong – Quantum Field Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 3: interactions as starting point; Subject relevance to lesson: M30-L5 – Path integrals, renormalization, and effective field theories · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M30-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M30-L6 – Dirac fields and quantum electrodynamics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Master: Quantum field theory and the Standard Model<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M30-L5: field interactions; M25: spin\n· <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish spinor from scalar\nfields and identify the structure of a QED vertex.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Electrons carry spin one half and obey the Pauli principle. They\ncannot be represented by the bosonic scalar field of M30-L1. The Dirac\nequation uses spinors and matrices; quantum electrodynamics (QED)\ncouples such charged fields to the electromagnetic gauge field.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>The Dirac matrices obey\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msup><mi>η<\/mi><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ν<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{\\gamma^\\mu,\\gamma^\\nu\\}=2\\eta^{\\mu\\nu}\\mathbf1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor signature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(+---)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA free spinor field obeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(i\\gamma^\\mu\\partial_\\mu-m)\\psi=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nElectromagnetic coupling is introduced by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+ieA_\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the electron-field convention chosen here; observable results do not\ndepend on a simultaneous arbitrary sign convention. The interaction\nproduces a vertex with two fermion legs and one photon leg.\nFermion-field excitations obey anticommutation relations.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msup><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>ψ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(i\\gamma^\\mu D_\\mu-m)\\psi=0,\\qquad D_\\mu=\\partial_\\mu+ieA_\\mu.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>At low energies the dimensionless fine-structure constant is\napproximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>137<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=e^2/(4\\pi)\\approx1/137<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin natural Heaviside–Lorentz units. A contribution with two\nelectromagnetic coupling factors in its amplitude is of order\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nbut its full rate still depends on kinematics and diagram structure. The\nfigure shows the elementary fermion–photon vertex.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_229_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"QED vertex showing a continuous directed fermion line meeting one wavy photon line.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">QED vertex showing a continuous directed\nfermion line meeting one wavy photon line.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>An elementary QED vertex couples two fermion legs to one photon\nleg.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which statistics do Dirac fermions follow?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How many fermion and photon legs meet at a fundamental QED\nvertex?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{\\gamma^0,\\gamma^0\\}=2\\mathbf1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Fermi–Dirac statistics with anticommutation relations.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Two fermion legs and one photon leg.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>γ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\gamma^0)^2=\\mathbf1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the chosen signature.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The drawn vertex is only the elementary QED interaction, not a\ncomplete experimental prediction. At high energies the coupling runs;\nprecision calculations also require regularization, renormalization, and\nwell-defined observables.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>M30-L7 extends the Abelian gauge idea to the non-Abelian strong\ninteraction. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/quantum-field-theory/\">Tong – Quantum Field Theory<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapters 4–6: Dirac equation, fermion quantization, QED; Subject relevance to lesson: M30-L6 – Dirac fields and quantum electrodynamics · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/standard-model/\">Tong – The Standard Model<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 1: gauge invariance as organizing principle; Subject relevance to lesson: M30-L6 – Dirac fields and quantum electrodynamics · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M30-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M30-L7 – Non-Abelian gauge theories and quantum chromodynamics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Master: Quantum field theory and the Standard Model<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M30-L6: QED; M30-L5: renormalization\ngroup · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Explain color charge and\ninterpret the one-loop running of the strong coupling qualitatively and\nnumerically.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Quarks have three color states, and eight gluons mediate the strong\ninteraction. Its gauge group is SU(3). Unlike electromagnetic U(1),\ngluons themselves carry color charge and interact with each other. This\nchanges how the coupling depends on energy scale.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>In the perturbative region and with fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nactive quark flavors, the leading running coupling is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>Q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>Q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_s(Q^2)=4\\pi/[\\beta_0\\ln(Q^2/\\Lambda^2)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_0=11-2n_f/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor SU(3). Positive\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields asymptotic freedom: the coupling becomes smaller at larger\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is a one-loop approximation;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndepends on convention and renormalization scheme. Perturbation theory\nceases to be reliable at low\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConfinement and hadronization require additional nonperturbative\nphysics.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>Q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>11<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>2<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>Q<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>≫<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_s(Q^2)=\\frac{4\\pi}{(11-\\tfrac23 n_f)\\ln(Q^2/\\Lambda^2)}\\qquad(Q\\gg\\Lambda).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>As an <strong>illustrative<\/strong> calculation with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_f=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q/\\Lambda=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none obtains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_0=9<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.303<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_s\\approx4\\pi/[9\\ln(100)]\\approx0.303<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q/\\Lambda=20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe same approximation gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.233<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_s\\approx0.233<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe curve shows the decrease, not precision experimental coupling values\nfor any particular experiment.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_228_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"One-loop curve of the decreasing strong coupling versus Q divided by Lambda in the perturbative region.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">One-loop curve of the decreasing strong\ncoupling versus Q divided by Lambda in the perturbative\nregion.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Strong coupling becomes weaker at higher energy scales in the\nperturbative regime.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_f=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbehave as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Q<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why must the one-loop curve not be extrapolated to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q=\\Lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas a measured result?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Nine.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It decreases in the perturbative region.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The denominator tends to zero there and perturbation theory becomes\nunreliable.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The numerical comparison holds\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n_f=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfixed across both illustrative scales; physical flavor thresholds\nrequire matching. Confinement cannot be derived from the simple one-loop\nexpression and is a nonperturbative property.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The Standard Model also contains the electroweak interaction and its\nHiggs mechanism. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/standard-model/\">Tong – The Standard Model<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapter 3: QCD and asymptotic freedom; Subject relevance to lesson: M30-L7 – Non-Abelian gauge theories and quantum chromodynamics · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/dbs26/AQFT.html\">Skinner – Quantum Field Theory II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Sections on non-Abelian gauge theory and beta functions; Subject relevance to lesson: M30-L7 – Non-Abelian gauge theories and quantum chromodynamics · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M30-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M30-L8 – Electroweak symmetry breaking and the Higgs mechanism<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Master: Quantum field theory and the Standard Model<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M30-L7: gauge symmetries; M30-L5:\neffective potentials · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish a symmetric\npotential from a chosen vacuum configuration and estimate the W-boson\nmass.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Electromagnetism and the weak interaction form the electroweak sector\nof the Standard Model. Its local gauge description remains valid even\nthough the Higgs field has a nonzero vacuum expectation value. The\nphysical W and Z bosons consequently acquire masses, while the photon\nremains massless.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>A simplified real slice through a symmetric potential is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>φ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(\\varphi)=\\lambda(\\varphi^2-v^2)^2/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lambda&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe potential is invariant under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>↦<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>φ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi\\mapsto-\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbut has minima at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi=\\pm v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis illustrates the choice of a vacuum configuration, but is\n<strong>not<\/strong> the full gauge-invariant Higgs-doublet theory. In\nthe Standard Model, the vacuum expectation value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>246<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v\\approx246\\,\\mathrm{GeV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives a mass term to the charged W boson through the Higgs-doublet\nkinetic term. Local gauge freedom is a redundancy of field variables,\nnot an ordinary physical global symmetry literally broken by the\nvacuum.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ℏ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_W=\\frac{gv}{2}\\qquad(\\hbar=c=1).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Using\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.65<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g\\approx0.65<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>246<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v\\approx246\\,\\mathrm{GeV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m<\/mi><mi>W<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.65<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>246<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>79.95<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m_W\\approx0.65\\times246/2\\approx79.95\\,\\mathrm{GeV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe sketch of a double-well potential shows a <strong>real toy-model\nslice<\/strong> with minima at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>±<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pm v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot the topology of the full electroweak Higgs-field space.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_131_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Symmetric double-well potential as a simplified real slice with two minima at minus v and plus v.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Symmetric double-well potential as a\nsimplified real slice with two minima at minus v and plus\nv.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Symmetric dynamics can allow several equivalent candidate vacuum\nconfigurations.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Where are the minima of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>λ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>φ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><msup><mi>v<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(\\varphi)=\\lambda(\\varphi^2-v^2)^2/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Evaluate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">gv/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.65<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=0.65<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>246<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v=246<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is the drawn double well the full Higgs-doublet potential?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi=-v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mi>v<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi=+v<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>79.95<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">79.95\\,\\mathrm{GeV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No, only a simplified real slice illustrating vacuum selection.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The chosen value of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a rough scale-specific approximation; precision predictions include\nradiative corrections and a specified renormalization scheme. The double\nwell is only an analogy, not a plot of the full gauge-orbit\nstructure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>M30-L9 addresses fermion masses, flavor mixing, and neutrino\noscillations. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L9&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/standard-model/\">Tong – The Standard Model<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapters 2 and 5: broken symmetries, Higgs mechanism, electroweak sector; Subject relevance to lesson: M30-L8 – Electroweak symmetry breaking and the Higgs mechanism · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/dbs26/AQFT.html\">Skinner – Quantum Field Theory II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Outline: gauge theory and effective field theory; Subject relevance to lesson: M30-L8 – Electroweak symmetry breaking and the Higgs mechanism · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M30-L9":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L9\">M30-L9 – Flavor mixing and neutrino oscillations<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Master: Quantum field theory and the Standard Model<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M30-L8: electroweak sector; M25:\nsuperposition · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish flavor and mass\neigenstates and calculate the two-flavor oscillation probability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Weak processes produce neutrinos in flavor states that are generally\nsuperpositions of distinct mass eigenstates. Different phases accumulate\nduring propagation, changing which flavor may later be detected. A\nrelated distinction between flavor and mass bases occurs for quarks\nthrough the CKM matrix; neutrino mixing uses the PMNS matrix.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>In a simplified two-flavor vacuum model,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\nu_a\\rangle=\\cos\\theta|\\nu_1\\rangle+\\sin\\theta|\\nu_2\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the orthogonal state is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|\\nu_b\\rangle=-\\sin\\theta|\\nu_1\\rangle+\\cos\\theta|\\nu_2\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHighly relativistic propagation leads to an approximate phase difference\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta m^2L/(2E)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a transition probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{a\\to b}=\\sin^2(2\\theta)\\sin^2(\\Delta m^2L/(4E))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn common laboratory units, the argument of the sine has a conversion\nfactor of approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>1.267<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.267<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1.267<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{a\\to b}=\\sin^2(2\\theta)\\sin^2\\!\\left(1.267\\,\\frac{\\Delta m^2\\,[\\mathrm{eV}^2]L\\,[\\mathrm{km}]}{E\\,[\\mathrm{GeV}]}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For the <strong>teaching-only two-flavor model<\/strong> with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2.5<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta m^2=2.5\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{eV}^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>295<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=295\\,\\mathrm{km}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.60<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E=0.60\\,\\mathrm{GeV}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe argument is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.557<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\approx1.557<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>θ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2(2\\theta)=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe probability is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>a<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.750<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{a\\to b}\\approx0.750<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is explicitly <strong>not<\/strong> a complete prediction for an\nactual\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/msub><mo>→<\/mo><msub><mi>ν<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nu_\\mu\\to\\nu_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nexperiment.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_188_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Oscillating transition probability between zero and a maximum of 0.75 for a fixed mixing angle.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Oscillating transition probability\nbetween zero and a maximum of 0.75 for a fixed mixing\nangle.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>In the two-flavor approximation, transition probability\noscillates with baseline divided by energy.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the maximum possible probability at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>30<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\theta=30^\\circ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What happens at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the two-flavor model insufficient for precision\nexperiments?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sin<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mn>60<\/mn><mo>∘<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sin^2(60^\\circ)=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The transition probability is zero.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Three flavors, CP phases, and matter effects can matter.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The formula assumes coherent propagation of ultrarelativistic\nneutrinos in vacuum and effectively two flavors. Real precision analyses\nuse the three-dimensional PMNS matrix, mass spectrum, possible CP\nviolation, and matter effects. The toy formula does not determine\nabsolute neutrino masses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Flavor mixing closes this introductory field-theory and\nStandard-Model sequence.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://davidtong.org/teaching/standard-model/\">Tong – The Standard Model<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Chapters 6–7: flavor mixing, neutrino masses and oscillations; Subject relevance to lesson: M30-L9 – Flavor mixing and neutrino oscillations · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.damtp.cam.ac.uk/user/dbs26/AQFT.html\">Skinner – Quantum Field Theory II<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Advanced context: effective theories and symmetries; Subject relevance to lesson: M30-L9 – Flavor mixing and neutrino oscillations · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
